## Protocol Boundary

Membership contains one anchor authority and exactly three witness identities,
all with distinct Ed25519 public keys. Endpoints are separate, replaceable
routing data and are not member identity. Each service stores one canonical
signed high-water receipt per world. An anchor request contains the literal
contiguous epistemic-journal segment, its start and end coordinates, and
domain-separated SHA-256 digests, then binds that payload with the configured
anchor signature. The service verifies this authorization before state access,
reparses every event with the same journal verifier used locally, requires its
own persisted `(count, head)` predecessor, and atomically persists the new
receipt before replying. The witness-signed receipt carries the anchor key ID,
request payload digest, and anchor signature for independent re-verification.

The client accepts two or three shares over one fixed membership, world,
anchor sequence, event count, and journal head. Its local certificate also
binds the preceding certificate digest and preceding checkpoint coordinates.
Local certificate self-consistency is insufficient. `crash-quorum` asks the
external services for current signed status and requires 2-of-3 receipts equal
to the latest local checkpoint under a non-equivocation assumption.
`byzantine-strict` requires all 3-of-3 current receipts and is the default CLI
policy.

Any two subsets of size two in a fixed set of three intersect, but their only
common member may be dishonest: `{B,H1}` and `{B,H2}` intersect only at `B`.
Signatures cannot prove that member honest, so 2-of-3 verification has only the
crash/non-equivocating result. Requiring all three current receipts makes either
honest witness that participated in any 2-of-3 anchor visible during
`byzantine-strict` verification. A stale or equivocating dishonest response then
causes disagreement and refusal. This is still not consensus: if the dishonest
witness is silent, byzantine-strict loses availability; if two witnesses
collude, restore old state, or lose state and keys together, v0 has no safety
claim.

## Crash And Catch-Up Semantics

The request digest makes an exact retry of the latest service request
idempotent. Retrying an older sequence is refused as non-monotonic. A harder crash can
occur after one or more services persist the next checkpoint but before the
client writes its certificate. On restart, the client treats a signed next
sequence as a recoverable candidate, verifies that its head is a prefix of the
local journal, completes the same checkpoint at another witness when needed,
and reconstructs the missing local certificate. It never asks an external
witness to move backward.

A lagging service may skip certificate sequence numbers because it validates
the full missing raw suffix from its own last head to the common new head. This
is state catch-up under fixed membership, not membership reconfiguration or
state transfer by assertion.

## Transport And Custody

V0 uses framed TCP bounded to 4 MiB, with raw journal segments bounded to 1 MiB.
Every state-advancing request is anchor-authorized and every receipt and
membership identity is signed and verified, so an active transport intermediary
cannot advance witness state or forge an accepted checkpoint without a
configured private key. It can still observe traffic, replay a request, delay
it, drop it, or cause denial of service. Exact latest-request replay is
idempotent; old replay is non-monotonic and refused. TLS, peer-channel
authentication, encryption, remote attestation, certificate rotation, and
hardware-backed keys are deliberately future interfaces.

The three witness state directories and private keys must not share the
rollback authority of the protected Loom state. Running all three under one
operator, filesystem snapshot, host, or credential vault may be useful for
testing but does not establish failure-domain or organizational independence.

## Native Rules And Linear Proof

Frame `9013` has five independent sabotage controls:

- `witness_mesh_members_are_separated` rejects repeated member identities;
- `witness_mesh_quorum_is_satisfied` requires canonical flags and two shares;
- `witness_mesh_receipts_match_membership` binds each share to its fixed slot
  and exact membership digest;
- `witness_mesh_checkpoint_agrees` binds domain, sequence, count, and head;
- `witness_mesh_checkpoint_advances` requires both coordinates to increase
  strictly: `anchor_sequence' > anchor_sequence` and
  `event_count' > event_count`.

Two private `VerifiedWitnessShare` values from distinct members are consumed to
construct one `VerifiedWitnessMeshCheckpoint`, which is consumed once to obtain
a private `HostWitnessMeshReceipt`. External construction refuses with `E176`;
share reuse refuses with `E039`.

## Evidence Boundary

The strongest v0 statement is conditional and exact: `byzantine-strict`
detects rollback or forking at or before the latest witnessed checkpoint with
at most one dishonest witness when all three services return current verifiable
status. `crash-quorum` provides the analogous 2-of-3 result only under
non-equivocation and state retention. An appended but unanchored suffix is
refused by either verifier, but it is not rollback-protected until an anchor
quorum signs it. Witnesses attest authorization, journal continuity, and
authorship of receipts, not the scientific truth, causal validity, safety, or
morality of journal content. Continuity evidence alone MUST NOT authorize a
clinical, dosing, classifier, or other consequential decision.

## Falsification Conditions

- a service accepts a segment whose first event does not extend its persisted
  predecessor or whose event hashes do not reach the claimed head;
- an unauthenticated or incorrectly signed anchor request advances state;
- an exact retry of the latest request changes state or returns a different
  signed receipt;
- a crash after external quorum persistence requires resetting a witness or
  loses the accepted checkpoint;
- one share can mint a local certificate or pass either verification policy;
- 2-of-3 crash-quorum passes after one member equivocates;
- 3-of-3 byzantine-strict passes a rollback view produced by one dishonest
  witness and one lagging honest witness;
- a locally hash-consistent rollback or fork passes strict verification against
  the advanced external witnesses;
- a lagging witness rejoins by trusting a claimed head without replaying its
  missing raw journal suffix;
- a certificate remains valid after anchor authority, membership, domain,
  count, sequence, head, request authorization, or a witness signature changes;
- removing one named frame-9013 rule does not admit its paired same-frame
  sabotage control;
- a report promotes the bounded result into Byzantine consensus, physical
  truth, organizational independence, or protection beyond the latest
  externally witnessed checkpoint.
